xnu 0343: probe the Bluetooth engine (QUPv3 wrap2 SE5) with its clocks voted

Item 17's driver: the engine's identity with its clocks voted, then the UART
programmed for 8N1 with a loopback byte and HCI_Reset on the wire. No QCA
firmware is loaded, and the loopback is what separates "the engine cannot frame
a byte" from "the chip does not answer": measured, the byte comes back as 5a
and the HCI_Reset completes with an empty RX FIFO, because the chip's rails and
reset GPIO are not in the kernel yet.

Four things in that sequence are not obvious and each cost a boot: the CTS mask
in SE_UART_TX_TRANS_CFG must be set with no flow control, the TX command starts
before the FIFO is filled, SE_GENI_BYTE_GRAN must be 0 for 8-bit words, and the
depths come from SE_HW_PARAM_0/1. They are recorded in
docs/MONDIAN_WIRELESS.md.

The addresses are the live tree's (docs/MONDIAN_WIRELESS.md): the engine is
qcom,msm-geni-serial-hs at 0x894000, which is SE5 of the wrapper at 0x8C0000
(SE apertures from 0x880000 in steps of 0x4000), with GIC SPI 587 and a wakeup
on GPIO 79. The console UART is the other engine (0x99c000, SPI 608).

The three clocks the node names resolve, in mainline's gcc-sm8450, to
GCC_QUPV3_WRAP2_S5_CLK, GCC_QUPV3_WRAP_2_M_AHB_CLK and
GCC_QUPV3_WRAP_2_S_AHB_CLK: ids 0x82, 0x8a and 0x8b, all bits of the one vote
register 0x62010 (SE5 bit 9, m-ahb 2, s-ahb 1, core 0, core 2x 3), with the SE's
own root at CMD_RCGR 0x2e618 / CFG_RCGR 0x2e61c. The root is set to the XO only
when the bootloader left it off, the way 0120 does it for the touch engine.

`btuart` on the control channel runs it and answers with the vote, the halt
state and the engine's identity registers; `btuart loop` programs the engine
for 8N1 and waits for a byte on its own RX, and `btuart hci` sends HCI_Reset
(01 03 0c 00) and prints what answers. Every line is flushed before the next
read, so a stalled bus leaves the culprit register on the ring.

